Date 03/11/2026

First assembly and movement successful!

Assembled K1, my first physical robot, including the chassis, servo motors, wheels and controller.
Successfully powered up the pico and connected it to my PC.
Installed and configured Thonny for CircuitPython.
Resolved missing Python library dependencies, including neapixel and adafruit_motor.
Established communication with K1 using python.
Wrote and successfully executed my first motor-control program.
Troubleshot an intermittent motor connection.
Experimented with motor speeds to improve straight-line movement.

My first program successfully controlled K1's motors, although I accidentally programmed it to drive backwards! 
After correcting the direction, I noticed K1 was veering slightly to the left.
Through experimentation, I found that the following motor speeds produced reasonably straight movement: car.set_speed(100, 95)
This taught me that two identical motors don't necessarily respond at exactly the same speed, and that software calibration can compensate for physical differences.

The biggest challenges were understanding some of the hardware connections, installing missing CircuitPython libraries, and troubleshooting a motor that initially refused to spin.
I learned how to investigate Python error messages, check electrical connections and isolate faults by swapping motor connections.

Today helped me understand the relationship between programming and physical hardware.
Unlike a simulated robot, K1's behaviour is affected by real-world factors such as motor differences, mechanical resistance and electrical connections.
Most importantly, I discovered how satisfying it is to write code and watch something I've physically built respond to it.

